{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Educational Technology Evaluation","title":"Classroom Fit Matrix","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting classroom fit matrix to an equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with gates applied above scored rows, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"05","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-01","statement":"Build an edtech evaluation matrix that applies pass-or-fail gates (privacy, accessibility, equity) before scored criteria, and record eliminated tools rather than averaging a must-have away"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_classroom_fit_matrix","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/05/04"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-03":["cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_ai_boundary"],"cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_artifact_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Classroom Fit Matrix\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Classroom Fit Matrix, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/05-05-classroom-fit-matrix.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Classroom Fit Matrix: Classroom Fit Matrix, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA fit matrix scores tools against weighted criteria and totals the points, which looks objective and often is not. The danger is structural: when a non-negotiable requirement is entered as one scored row among many, a tool can fail that requirement completely and still win on total points, because strengths elsewhere paper over the gap. A rural classroom that loses internet three times a week does not need a tool that scores high on average; it needs a tool that works offline, full stop. This section teaches the difference between a criterion you average and a constraint you gate, because confusing the two lets deal-breakers disappear into a sum.\n\nThe matrix is a reasoning tool, not an oracle. Its weights, its rows, and its choice of what to gate are all judgments the teacher makes and must defend. Building the matrix well is how a teacher turns \"it felt like the better tool\" into a decision a skeptic can audit row by row.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Distinguish a scored criterion (averaged into a total) from a gating constraint (pass or fail before scoring).\n- Recognize when summing a must-have into a total lets a low score be offset by unrelated strengths.\n- Reclassify a non-negotiable requirement as a hard gate and remove failing tools before they are scored.\n- Document a fit decision in which a high total was overruled by a failed gate, with the reasoning shown.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA rural classroom with unreliable internet builds a fit matrix for two collaboration tools, weighting usability, features, accessibility, and offline support. Tool A scores high on usability and features and posts the higher weighted total; Tool B is plainer but reliable offline. The team is ready to pick Tool A \"because it scored higher,\" even though the building loses connectivity several times a week.\n\nThe decision worth recording is not \"Tool A won the total.\" It is whether the team recognized that offline support is a gate, not a row, and that a tool which cannot deliver the lesson when the wifi drops should never have been scored against a tool that can.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA fit matrix has two kinds of rows, and treating them the same is the central error:\n\n- A **scored criterion** is a quality where more is better and trade-offs are legitimate. Usability, feature depth, and visual design belong here; a weak score on one can be reasonably offset by a strong score on another, because none is essential by itself.\n- A **gating constraint** is a requirement the tool must meet to function in your context at all. Offline capability in a low-connectivity building, screen-reader access for a student who needs it, a signed data agreement before student accounts exist: these are pass or fail. A tool that fails the gate cannot be rescued by points elsewhere, because the points buy nothing if the lesson cannot run.\n\nThe mechanism of the trap is **compensatory scoring**. When a must-have is entered as a 1-to-5 row, a score of 1 on that row can be outweighed by 5s elsewhere, and the total reports a winner that fails the one thing that mattered. The fix is **non-compensatory gating**: move the must-have out of the scored rows, apply it as a filter, and remove any tool that fails before computing any totals. (The compensatory versus non-compensatory distinction is standard multi-criteria decision analysis, not a course invention; see Cinelli et al., 2020, for the formal taxonomy.)\n\nA short procedure makes it operational:\n\n1. Separate rows into gates and scored criteria.\n2. Apply every gate first; eliminate tools that fail any gate.\n3. Score the survivors on the remaining criteria and total the points.\n4. Record which tools were gated out and why, so the total is read only among eligible tools.\n\nThe explorer shows the trap and the fix on one screen. Score Tool A on usability, features, and accessibility, then set its offline-support score and a gate flag. The compensatory total averages all four rows, so strong rows hide a weak must-have. The gated total drops to zero the moment offline support falls below the pass line you set, because a tool that fails the gate buys nothing with points elsewhere. (The gate term multiplies the total by 1 when offline meets the threshold and by 0 when it does not.)\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Compensatory Total Versus Non-Compensatory Gate\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"usability\",\"label\":\"Usability score\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"pts\",\"default\":5},{\"name\":\"features\",\"label\":\"Features score\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"pts\",\"default\":5},{\"name\":\"access\",\"label\":\"Accessibility score\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"pts\",\"default\":4},{\"name\":\"offline\",\"label\":\"Offline-support score\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"pts\",\"default\":1},{\"name\":\"gateline\",\"label\":\"Gate pass line for offline support\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"pts\",\"default\":3}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Compensatory total (offline averaged in, percent of 20)\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"(usability + features + access + offline) / 20 * 100\"},{\"label\":\"Does the tool pass the offline gate? (1 = yes, 0 = no)\",\"unit\":\"\",\"expr\":\"(1 + (offline - gateline) / sqrt((offline - gateline)^2 + 0.01)) / 2\"},{\"label\":\"Gated total (zero if the gate fails)\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"(usability + features + access + offline) / 20 * 100 * (1 + (offline - gateline) / sqrt((offline - gateline)^2 + 0.01)) / 2\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"offline\",\"output\":2}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check question=\"In a rural building that loses internet several times a week, Tool A scores usability 5, features 5, accessibility 4, offline 1, for a high compensatory total. Tool B scores lower elsewhere but offline 5. Why does Tool A's high total mislead?\" explanation=\"Because offline support was entered as a scored row that strong usability and features could offset. In this building, a tool that cannot run offline cannot deliver the lesson at all, so offline is a gate, not a criterion. Once it is gated, Tool A is eliminated before scoring and its high total never enters the decision.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It does not mislead; the higher total is the better tool.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Offline support is a gate here, not a scored row; Tool A should be eliminated before any total is computed.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Tool A is better because accessibility scored a 4.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThree course anchors discipline the matrix. **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** decides which criteria reflect real learning value versus surface features, so weights are not arbitrary. **Critical edtech studies** (Selwyn, 2016) helps identify which requirements are non-negotiable for your students rather than for the median classroom. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** often supplies a gate of its own: a privacy or data-handling line a high feature score cannot purchase past, typically a signed data-privacy agreement consistent with FERPA (20 U.S.C. 1232g) and, for under-13 students, the amended COPPA Rule, before any student accounts exist.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA first draft often hides the real decision behind a vague improvement claim:\n\n> I made the evaluation matrix clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the edtech evaluation matrix so the must-have rows are gates applied before any scoring: the first pass had averaged offline support into the total, which let Tool A's polish buy back a deal-breaker, so the revision moves offline (and privacy) above the scored rows and records the eliminated tool. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact rows that changed.\n\nNotice the shift: the artifact is no longer just polished. The revision names the evidence source and preserves the reason a suggestion was accepted or rejected.\n\n### Worked Example: When a Total Hides the Deal-Breaker\n\nScenario: a rural classroom with unreliable internet builds a fit matrix for two collaboration tools, weighting four criteria.\n\n1. **First pass (before).** The team scores each tool 1 to 5 and weights the rows: usability 40 percent, features 35 percent, accessibility 20 percent, offline support 5 percent. Tool A: usability 5, features 5, accessibility 4, offline support 1, weighted total 92 percent (40 + 35 + 16 + 1). Tool B: usability 4, features 4, accessibility 4, offline support 5, weighted total 81 percent (32 + 28 + 16 + 5). Tool B's raw total (17 of 20) actually beats Tool A's (15 of 20), but the weights hand Tool A the win. The note reads \"A wins, higher percent.\"\n2. **Found the gating constraint.** Offline support is not a nice-to-have here; the building loses internet several times a week. A tool scoring 1 on offline support cannot deliver the lesson at all.\n3. **Reweighted as a gate.** The team moved offline support from a scored row to a pass/fail gate. Tool A fails the gate and is removed before scoring. Tool B passes, so it is the only candidate.\n4. **Annotated judgment call (after).** The revised matrix records: \"Offline support reclassified from a 1-to-5 criterion to a hard gate because of building connectivity. Tool A's 92 percent is irrelevant once it fails the gate. We rejected averaging a must-have into the total because it let a deal-breaker disappear.\"\n\nThe matrix is defensible because the non-negotiable constraint now controls the decision instead of being diluted by points it could never offset.\n\nWalk the rural matrix through the wrong way and the fix, predicting each step before it reveals.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Rebuilding the rural-classroom matrix so the deal-breaker cannot hide\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You score offline support as a 1-to-5 row. Tool A: usability 5, features 5, accessibility 4, offline 1. Tool B: 4, 4, 4, offline 5. The building loses internet several times a week. What does the compensatory total report?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Tool B wins because offline is most important\",\"Tool A can post a competitive or higher total because its strong rows offset the 1\",\"Both tools are disqualified automatically\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Tool A’s strong usability and features offset its offline score of 1, so the weighted total carries it past Tool B (92 percent to 81 percent). The sum is arithmetically correct and practically false: it ranks a tool that cannot run when the wifi drops.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Is offline support a quality to trade off, or a condition of the tool working at all in this building?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A quality to trade off, like visual design\",\"A condition of the tool working; without it the lesson cannot run\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"In a building that drops connectivity several times a week, offline capability is the condition of the tool functioning. That makes it a gate, not a scored criterion. Any weight you give it as a row can still be outscored.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You reclassify offline support as a pass/fail gate and apply it before scoring. What happens to Tool A?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It is rescored with a heavier weight\",\"It fails the gate and is removed before any total is computed\",\"Its total is rounded down\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Tool A fails the gate and leaves the comparison before scoring. Its 92 percent never enters the decision because it was never eligible. Only Tool B, which passes the gate, is scored.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Record the structure plainly: ’Offline support reclassified from a scored row to a gate because of building connectivity; Tool A eliminated at the gate; Tool B scored among survivors.’ A reviewer now sees the decision turned on the requirement that governs the classroom, not on points it could never offset.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nUse the gating constraints first, the scored criteria second, and the total last. A tool is eligible for scoring only after it passes every gate; a high total among ineligible tools is not a finding.\n\nBefore you compute any total, audit the matrix structure:\n\n1. **Gate check:** Which rows are requirements the tool must meet to function here at all? Pull them out of the scored rows.\n2. **Elimination check:** Does any tool fail a gate? Remove it before scoring, and record why.\n3. **Total check:** Among the survivors, does the weighted total reflect criteria where trade-offs are genuinely acceptable?\n\nIf a single low score on one row would be a deal-breaker in your classroom, that row is a gate, not a criterion, and leaving it in the sum is the error to fix.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is trusting the total because it looks numeric and therefore objective; a sum is only as sound as the decision to make every row compensatory. The second is entering a must-have as a weighted row and assuming a heavy weight protects it, when in fact any weight can be outscored if the row is averaged rather than gated. The third is reporting a winner without recording which tools were eliminated by gates, leaving a reader unable to see that the total was computed among eligible tools only.\n\nA defensible matrix shows the gates above the scored rows, lists the tools each gate removed, and computes totals only for the survivors.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nBuild the rural-classroom matrix the wrong way first so the trap is visible. With offline support as a scored 1-to-5 row, Tool A's strong usability and features carry it to the higher weighted total even though it scores a 1 on the row that decides whether the lesson can run at all. The sum is arithmetically correct and practically false.\n\nNow find the gate. Offline support in a building that drops connectivity several times a week is not a quality to be traded off; it is the condition of the tool working. Pull it out of the scored rows and apply it as pass or fail. Tool A fails and leaves the comparison before any total is computed; its 92 percent never enters the decision because it was never eligible.\n\nScore only the survivor and record the structure plainly: \"Offline support reclassified from a scored row to a gate because of building connectivity; Tool A eliminated at the gate; Tool B scored among survivors.\" A reviewer can now see that the decision turned on the requirement that actually governs the classroom, not on points the requirement could never have offset.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-list note: **gates** on one side, **scored criteria** on the other. For every row a tempting matrix would average, ask whether a failing score on it would actually end the decision in your room. If it would, the row belongs on the gates list, and the exercise reveals how often a deal-breaker has been quietly filed as a tradeable criterion.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which rows are gates and which are scored criteria, and why?\n2. Where could a high total hide a failed must-have through compensatory scoring?\n3. Which tools should be eliminated before scoring, and what is the recorded reason?\n4. Is the reported total computed only among tools that passed every gate?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nFor this section, the matrix is strong when gates are listed and applied above the scored rows, eliminated tools are recorded with reasons, and the weighted total appears only for survivors, so a mentor can confirm no deal-breaker was averaged away. Treat a high total for a tool that failed a gate as a red flag, not a recommendation.\n\nA useful closing line would sound like this: \"I changed the matrix so offline support is a pass/fail gate, not a scored row, which eliminates Tool A before scoring. I kept out the suggestion to keep it as a weighted criterion because any weight still let Tool A's other strengths offset a deal-breaker.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same two tools to a one-to-one suburban school with rock-solid wifi. Offline support is no longer a gate; the building never loses connectivity, so a tool that fails offline loses nothing operational. Offline support drops back to a scored criterion, or off the matrix entirely, and Tool A's higher total may now be a legitimate winner. The gates list is context-specific by design: a requirement that gates one classroom is a mere preference in another. Use the shift to confirm that you can re-sort gates and criteria for a new setting rather than carrying the rural verdict into a connected one.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/05-05-classroom-fit-matrix-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with gates applied before scored rows\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"05.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix for CAT 531 05.05. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation\",\"cat531_supplement_classroom_fit_matrix\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_classroom_fit_matrix\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm the matrix separates gating constraints from scored criteria, applies the gates first and eliminates failing tools before scoring, records why each tool was removed, and reports a total only for the tools that passed every gate.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning et al. (2024), Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Selwyn, N. (2016), Is Technology Good for Education? Polity, ISBN 9780745696478 (critical edtech studies).\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- Williamson, B., Macgilchrist, F., & Potter, J. (2023), Re-examining AI, automation and datafication in education. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2023.2167830\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_classroom_fit_matrix","stem":"A classroom fit matrix scores each tool on a list of criteria. What makes a fit matrix more than a generic feature checklist?","options":["It uses more rows than a checklist.","Its criteria and weights are tied to the specific classroom: the learners, the unit, and the constraints that actually matter here.","It assigns every tool a single overall percentage.","It copies the criteria from a published rubric without changes."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A fit matrix is contextual. The same tool can fit one classroom and not another, so the criteria and their weights must reflect this room's learners, unit, and constraints rather than a universal list.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_matrix_is_universal","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_classroom_fit_matrix","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_classroom_fit_matrix","stem":"Tool A scores 92 percent and Tool B scores 88 percent on a fit matrix. Tool B is the only one that works offline, which this rural classroom needs. What does this reveal about the totals?","options":["Tool A wins because 92 is higher than 88.","An aggregate score can hide a non-negotiable constraint; a deal-breaker criterion should gate the decision, not be averaged away.","The four-point gap proves Tool A is clearly better.","Offline access is a minor criterion that the total already handles."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Averaging treats a must-have like any other point. When a criterion is a hard constraint, it should act as a gate that removes non-compliant tools before scoring, or the total misleads.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_total_overrides_dealbreaker","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_classroom_fit_matrix"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to fill in your classroom fit matrix. It assigns plausible scores to every cell. What is the defensible move?","options":["Accept the AI scores since the matrix is now complete.","Treat the AI scores as hypotheses, then verify each one against the tool and your classroom before trusting any cell.","Ask the AI to also pick the winner.","Use the AI scores only for the criteria you dislike rating."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An AI cannot know your learners or test the tool in your room. Its scores are hypotheses to verify, not classroom evidence. The teacher owns the judgment behind each cell.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_ai_fills_matrix","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat531_supplement_classroom_fit_matrix"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_classroom_fit_matrix_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A colleague borrows your fit matrix for their classroom unchanged. Why might your high-fit tool be a poor fit for them?","options":["Fit scores are objective and transfer directly between classrooms.","Their learners, constraints, and instructional goals differ, so the weights and even the criteria likely need to change before the scores mean anything.","The tool changes between classrooms.","A fit matrix only works in the classroom that built it because of the file format."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Fit is relative to context. Reusing a matrix without re-examining the weights and criteria imports another room's priorities and produces a misleading verdict.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_fit_transfers_unchanged","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision","cat531_supplement_classroom_fit_matrix"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-05-05-lo-02"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_matrix_is_universal","pattern":"fit_matrix_as_universal_checklist","description":"Treating a classroom fit matrix as a universal feature checklist rather than a context-specific instrument","trigger":"learner reuses generic criteria and weights without tying them to this classroom","feedback":"Tie the criteria and weights to your learners, unit, and constraints. A fit matrix is contextual, not a universal checklist.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_total_overrides_dealbreaker","pattern":"aggregate_score_hides_dealbreaker","description":"Letting an aggregate fit score override a non-negotiable constraint that should gate the decision","trigger":"learner picks the higher total even though it fails a must-have criterion","feedback":"Make hard constraints gate the decision. A deal-breaker should remove a tool before scoring, not be averaged into the total.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_ai_fills_matrix","pattern":"ai_scores_treated_as_classroom_evidence","description":"Accepting AI-assigned fit-matrix cells as verified classroom evidence","trigger":"learner adopts AI-filled scores without verifying them against the tool and the room","feedback":"AI scores are hypotheses. Verify each cell against the actual tool and your classroom before trusting it.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_5_fit_transfers_unchanged","pattern":"fit_score_assumed_transferable","description":"Assuming a fit-matrix verdict transfers unchanged to a different classroom","trigger":"learner reuses another room's matrix without revisiting weights or criteria","feedback":"Fit is relative to context. Re-examine the criteria and weights for the new learners and constraints before reusing the verdict.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"aed183ea6e305aa97c8a705436d8795ad84f8aa183fdc5a221fb8bca97db3097","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}